@* RangeSlider's Overview, written by hand after Slider's: binding a NumberRange, filtering with
   it, naming the thumbs, what holds without script, vertical and states.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>A range slider picks a span, such as a price filter's lowest and highest prices. It's two native range inputs on one track, so both ends post with the form, <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too. When the exact numbers matter, use two <Anchor Href="docs/components/number-field">NumberFields</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Filtering by a range</Heading>
<TextBlock>Bind the slider to a <Code>NumberRange</Code> of any number type, such as <Code>NumberRange&lt;int&gt;</Code> or <Code>NumberRange&lt;decimal&gt;</Code>. It holds <Code>Start</Code> and <Code>End</Code>, both included, and <Code>Contains</Code> tests a value against them. The ends post as <Code>Price.Start</Code> and <Code>Price.End</Code>, so a <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm]</Code> model binds them on a static page.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The scale runs from 0 to 100 by default. To change it, set <Code>Min</Code> and <Code>Max</Code>; to set the increments, set <Code>Step</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="range-slider-usage" />
<TextBlock>To show each thumb's value in a tooltip over it, set <Code>ValueTooltip</Code> to <Code>SliderTooltip.Active</Code> (while it's hovered, focused or dragged) or <Code>SliderTooltip.Always</Code>. To label the ends of the track, set <Code>ShowEnds</Code>; for marks, set <Code>Marks</Code> or <Code>ShowStepMarks</Code>, as on <Anchor Href="docs/components/slider">Slider</Anchor>. To set how values read, such as <Code>"${0}"</Code>, set <Code>ValueFormat</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>With no value, the thumbs sit at the ends of the scale, as a filter that hasn't narrowed anything.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Naming the thumbs</Heading>
<TextBlock>Screen readers hear the label and then each thumb's name, "Minimum" and "Maximum" by default. When other words fit better, such as "Earliest" and "Latest", set <Code>StartText</Code> and <Code>EndText</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="range-slider-window" />

<Heading Level="2">When the thumbs meet</Heading>
<TextBlock>With <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor>, a thumb stops at the other, so the ends can be equal but never cross, and a click on the track moves the nearer thumb there. With JavaScript off, the thumbs can pass each other; when the form posts, the server binds the lower value as <Code>Start</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The server rejects an end outside <Code>Min</Code> and <Code>Max</Code>, so a crafted post can't widen the range. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Vertical, live and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To stand the slider upright, set <Code>Orientation</Code> to <Code>Orientation.Vertical</Code>; the lowest value is at the bottom, and <Code>Height</Code> sets the track's length, 10rem by default. On interactive pages, to update the bound value as a thumb moves, set <Code>Live</Code>; each move is a round trip. <Code>Size</Code> and <Code>Disabled</Code> work as on <Anchor Href="docs/components/slider">Slider</Anchor>, and so does a read-only <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>: the slider is disabled, and hidden fields post both ends.</TextBlock>

